// Domain 5: Data Protection (18%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d5-01", d: 5,
  q: "A public web application runs behind an Application Load Balancer that has only an HTTP listener on port 80. A new policy requires that all client connections use HTTPS with TLS 1.2 or later and that weaker protocols are refused. Users who type an http:// address must still reach the site, and some partner clients support TLS 1.2 but not TLS 1.3.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add an HTTPS listener with the ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06 security policy, and change the HTTP listener to redirect to HTTPS.",
      "Add an HTTPS listener with the ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-3-2021-06 security policy, and change the HTTP listener to redirect to HTTPS.",
      "Add an HTTPS listener with the ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06 security policy, and remove the HTTP listener from the load balancer.",
      "Add an HTTPS listener with the ELBSecurityPolicy-2016-08 security policy, and change the HTTP listener to redirect to HTTPS."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06 allows TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 only, which keeps the partners working and refuses older protocols. The redirect action sends http:// users to HTTPS.",
      "The TLS13-1-3 policy accepts only TLS 1.3, so the partner clients that support only TLS 1.2 cannot connect.",
      "Without an HTTP listener, users who type http:// get no response instead of a redirect.",
      "ELBSecurityPolicy-2016-08 also allows TLS 1.0 and 1.1, which the policy requires to be refused."] },

{ id: "d5-02", d: 5,
  q: "A security review finds that some internal applications send requests to a sensitive Amazon S3 bucket over plain HTTP. The bucket must reject every request that does not use HTTPS, from every principal, including principals in other accounts and principals whose IAM policies allow full S3 access.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy statement that denies all S3 actions for all principals when aws:SecureTransport is false.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that allows all S3 actions only when aws:SecureTransport is true, and remove the other allows.",
      "Add an SCP that denies all S3 actions when aws:SecureTransport is false, and attach it to the account that owns the bucket.",
      "Turn on S3 Block Public Access and default encryption with SSE-KMS for the bucket, and keep the bucket policy as it is."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["aws:SecureTransport is false for requests sent without TLS. An explicit deny in the bucket policy applies to every principal, whatever their IAM policies allow.",
      "An allow statement does not deny anything. Principals in the same account whose IAM policies allow S3 could still use HTTP.",
      "An SCP limits principals in the accounts it is attached to. It does not apply to principals from other accounts that call the bucket.",
      "Block Public Access and encryption at rest do not control whether requests use TLS."] },

{ id: "d5-03", d: 5,
  q: "A company's Amazon S3 bucket already denies requests that do not use HTTPS. A new compliance rule requires TLS 1.2 or later for every request to the bucket. Requests that negotiate TLS 1.0 or TLS 1.1 must be denied, and the rule must be enforced by AWS rather than by the clients.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy statement that denies all S3 actions when the s3:TlsVersion condition key is less than 1.2, with NumericLessThan.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that denies all S3 actions when the s3:TlsVersion condition key equals 1.2, with the NumericEquals operator.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that denies all S3 actions when the aws:SecureTransport condition key is true for the request.",
      "Set the minimum TLS version for the bucket to 1.2 in the bucket's properties, and keep the existing bucket policy as it is."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The s3:TlsVersion condition key holds the TLS version of the request. A deny with NumericLessThan 1.2 rejects TLS 1.0 and 1.1 for every caller.",
      "This denies TLS 1.2 itself and allows TLS 1.0 and 1.1, which is the opposite of the rule.",
      "Every HTTPS request has aws:SecureTransport set to true, so this denies all requests.",
      "S3 buckets have no minimum TLS version property. The bucket policy is where the version is enforced."] },

{ id: "d5-04", d: 5,
  q: "A company serves a web application through Amazon CloudFront with an Application Load Balancer as the custom origin. Traffic must be encrypted from viewers to CloudFront and from CloudFront to the origin. Viewers that use HTTP must be redirected to HTTPS, and the origin must not accept HTTP from CloudFront.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Set the viewer protocol policy on every cache behavior to Redirect HTTP to HTTPS.",
      "Set the origin protocol policy to HTTPS only, with a certificate on the load balancer for the origin name.",
      "Set the viewer protocol policy on every cache behavior to HTTPS only for all of the viewers.",
      "Set the origin protocol policy to Match viewer, and keep an HTTP listener on the load balancer.",
      "Turn on field-level encryption for the request fields that viewers send to the distribution."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Redirect HTTP to HTTPS sends HTTP viewers to HTTPS, so traffic between viewers and CloudFront is encrypted and http:// users still arrive.",
      "HTTPS only makes CloudFront connect to the origin over HTTPS, and the origin needs a certificate for the name that CloudFront uses.",
      "HTTPS only rejects HTTP viewers with an error instead of redirecting them.",
      "Keeping an HTTP listener on the origin breaks the requirement that the origin must not accept HTTP.",
      "Field-level encryption protects chosen fields. It does not encrypt the connections."] },

{ id: "d5-05", d: 5,
  q: "A company exposes an API to 40 partner systems through an Application Load Balancer. Each partner has a client certificate issued by the company's private certificate authority. The load balancer itself must reject connections from clients without a valid certificate from that authority, and certificates that the company revokes must stop working.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Configure mutual TLS in verify mode on the HTTPS listener, with a trust store that holds the CA certificate and its revocation list.",
      "Configure mutual TLS in passthrough mode on the HTTPS listener, and have the targets check the client certificate headers that they receive.",
      "Configure mutual TLS in verify mode on the HTTPS listener, with a trust store that holds only the CA certificate and no revocation list.",
      "Add an AWS WAF rule that allows only requests that include the X-Amzn-Mtls-Clientcert header, and keep server-only TLS on the listener."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["In verify mode, the load balancer authenticates each client certificate against the trust store. A certificate revocation list in the trust store makes revoked certificates fail.",
      "In passthrough mode, the load balancer forwards the certificate without checking it, so the load balancer does not reject anything.",
      "Without a revocation list, revoked certificates still pass validation.",
      "That header is added only in passthrough mode, and a header check is not certificate authentication."] },

{ id: "d5-06", d: 5,
  q: "A public certificate from AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) on an Application Load Balancer was issued with DNS validation 11 months ago. ACM now shows the certificate as ineligible for automatic renewal. Last month, the DNS team removed records that it considered unused from the domain's Amazon Route 53 hosted zone.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The CNAME record that ACM created for DNS validation was removed, so ACM cannot confirm control of the domain to renew it.",
      "The certificate is associated with a load balancer, and ACM renews only certificates that are not associated with any resource.",
      "The certificate uses an RSA 2048 key, and ACM no longer renews RSA certificates automatically when they are on load balancers.",
      "ACM renews DNS-validated certificates only when DNSSEC signing is turned on for the hosted zone that holds the CNAME record."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ACM renews DNS-validated certificates automatically while the validation CNAME record stays in DNS. With the record removed, ACM cannot confirm control of the domain, so the team must add it back.",
      "ACM renews certificates that are in use with integrated services such as load balancers.",
      "ACM renews RSA 2048 certificates automatically.",
      "DNSSEC is not required for ACM DNS validation or renewal."] },

{ id: "d5-07", d: 5,
  q: "A company runs 300 internal microservices that talk to each other over TLS. The services need certificates for private DNS names such as orders.internal.example.com, and the certificates must renew automatically. The company does not want to run certificate authority software or manage CA private keys itself.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a CA in AWS Private Certificate Authority, and request private certificates for the services through ACM.",
      "Create a CA in AWS Private Certificate Authority, and issue the certificates from the CA's API without ACM.",
      "Run an open source certificate authority on Amazon EC2, and store its private key in AWS Secrets Manager.",
      "Create self-signed certificates on each service, and add each certificate to the trust store of every other service."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Private CA is a managed private certificate authority that keeps the CA keys for the company. Private certificates requested through ACM are renewed by ACM automatically.",
      "Certificates issued directly from the Private CA API are not managed by ACM, so the company must renew them itself.",
      "Running CA software and protecting its private key is the work that the company wants to avoid.",
      "Self-signed certificates must be distributed and renewed by hand across 300 services."] },

{ id: "d5-08", d: 5,
  q: "A company processes sensitive data with Apache Spark on Amazon EMR. Data that moves between the cluster's nodes must be encrypted in transit, data that EMR writes to Amazon S3 through EMRFS must use SSE-KMS, and the nodes' local disks must be encrypted. The company wants one reusable setting for many clusters.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an EMR security configuration with in-transit encryption, at-rest encryption for EMRFS with SSE-KMS, and local disk encryption. Use it for each cluster.",
      "Create an EMR security configuration with at-rest encryption for EMRFS with SSE-KMS and local disk encryption only. Use it for each of the clusters.",
      "Turn on default SSE-KMS encryption on the S3 buckets, and launch each cluster in a private subnet without a security configuration.",
      "Create an EMR security configuration with in-transit encryption only, and turn on EBS encryption by default for the account in the Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An EMR security configuration is a reusable setting that turns on in-transit encryption, EMRFS encryption with SSE-KMS, and local disk encryption for every cluster that uses it.",
      "Without in-transit encryption, the traffic between the nodes is not encrypted.",
      "Bucket defaults do not encrypt traffic between nodes or the local disks, and a private subnet does not encrypt traffic.",
      "EBS encryption by default does not cover instance store disks, and EMRFS writes are not set to SSE-KMS by the security configuration."] },

{ id: "d5-09", d: 5,
  q: "A data science team runs distributed training jobs in Amazon SageMaker AI across 8 instances in a VPC. The training data is sensitive, and the data that moves between the training containers during each job must be encrypted. The training data in Amazon S3 is already encrypted with SSE-KMS.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Turn on inter-container traffic encryption in the training job configuration.",
      "Set a KMS key for the training job's output data and the attached ML storage volumes.",
      "Run the training jobs in network isolation mode with no outbound internet access.",
      "Use an interface VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 to keep the training data on the AWS network."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Inter-container traffic encryption encrypts the data that moves between the containers of a distributed training job.",
      "These keys encrypt data at rest. They do not encrypt traffic between the containers.",
      "Network isolation blocks outside network access, but it does not encrypt traffic between the containers.",
      "An endpoint keeps S3 traffic private, but traffic between the training containers is still unencrypted."] },

{ id: "d5-10", d: 5,
  q: "A company runs a legacy application on Amazon EC2 instances that talk to each other over an unencrypted protocol inside one VPC. The application cannot be changed, and no software can be added to the instances. The security team must encrypt the traffic between the instances with the least effort.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Move the application to instance types that automatically encrypt in-transit traffic between Nitro instances in the same VPC.",
      "Move the application to instance types with enhanced networking, which encrypts all traffic between any two instance types in a VPC.",
      "Create an AWS Site-to-Site VPN between two subnets in the VPC, and route the application's traffic between them through the tunnels.",
      "Turn on VPC Flow Logs for the subnets, and alert the security team whenever traffic between the instances is not encrypted."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Some Nitro-based instance types automatically encrypt traffic between instances of supported types in the same VPC or a peered VPC, with no change to the application or the instances' software.",
      "Enhanced networking improves performance. Encryption in transit depends on supported instance types at both ends, not on enhanced networking.",
      "Site-to-Site VPN connects a VPC to a remote network. It cannot create tunnels between two subnets of the same VPC.",
      "Flow logs record traffic but do not encrypt it."] },

{ id: "d5-11", d: 5,
  q: "Linux instances mount an Amazon EFS file system that stores regulated data. A security review requires that all NFS traffic between clients and the file system is encrypted, and that the file system itself rejects any client that tries to mount it without encryption in transit.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Mount the file system on the instances with the Amazon EFS mount helper and the tls option.",
      "Add a file system policy that denies all access when aws:SecureTransport is false.",
      "Turn on encryption at rest for the file system with a customer managed KMS key.",
      "Allow only TCP 2049 from the instances in the security group of the mount targets.",
      "Mount the file system with the standard NFS client and the sec=krb5p option."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The EFS mount helper with the tls option encrypts the NFS traffic between the client and the file system with TLS.",
      "A file system policy that denies requests without secure transport makes EFS reject clients that mount without TLS.",
      "Encryption at rest protects stored data, not traffic.",
      "Security groups control which clients can connect, not whether the traffic is encrypted.",
      "Amazon EFS does not support Kerberos security options."] },

{ id: "d5-12", d: 5,
  q: "A company stores security logs in an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain. A security review requires that traffic between the domain's nodes is encrypted, that clients connect only over HTTPS with TLS 1.2 or later, and that the data at rest is encrypted with an AWS KMS key.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on node-to-node encryption, encryption at rest, and Require HTTPS with a TLS security policy that allows only TLS 1.2 or later.",
      "Turn on encryption at rest and Require HTTPS with a TLS security policy that allows only TLS 1.2 or later, and place the domain in a VPC.",
      "Turn on node-to-node encryption and encryption at rest, and put an Application Load Balancer with an HTTPS listener in front of the domain.",
      "Turn on node-to-node encryption and Require HTTPS, and encrypt each document on the clients before it is indexed in the domain."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Node-to-node encryption covers traffic between nodes, Require HTTPS with a TLS 1.2 policy covers clients, and encryption at rest uses a KMS key for stored data.",
      "Without node-to-node encryption, the traffic between the nodes is not encrypted, and a VPC does not encrypt it.",
      "Clients can still connect to the domain endpoint directly without HTTPS, because nothing on the domain requires it.",
      "Client-side encryption is not encryption at rest with a KMS key on the domain, and it breaks searching the logs."] },

{ id: "d5-13", d: 5,
  q: "A security engineer changes an Application Load Balancer listener's security policy from ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06 to ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-3-2021-06. Afterward, an older partner system that supports TLS 1.2 but not TLS 1.3 fails the TLS handshake, while modern browsers still connect. TLS 1.0 and 1.1 must stay disabled.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Change the listener back to a policy that supports TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3, such as ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06.",
      "Change the listener to ELBSecurityPolicy-2016-08, which supports every TLS version that the partner system might use.",
      "Add the partner system's IP address to the load balancer's security group, and keep the TLS 1.3 only security policy.",
      "Replace the listener's certificate with an ECDSA certificate, which TLS 1.2 clients can use with the TLS 1.3 only policy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The TLS13-1-3 policy accepts only TLS 1.3. A policy that supports TLS 1.2 and 1.3 lets the partner connect while TLS 1.0 and 1.1 stay disabled.",
      "ELBSecurityPolicy-2016-08 also turns TLS 1.0 and 1.1 back on, which is not allowed.",
      "The handshake fails because of the protocol version, not because of network access.",
      "The certificate type does not change which protocol versions the listener accepts."] },

{ id: "d5-14", d: 5,
  q: "A payment application must store and use its encryption keys only in single-tenant, FIPS-validated hardware security modules (HSMs) in AWS that the company controls exclusively, with no AWS administrator able to use the keys. The application calls the HSMs through the PKCS #11 interface.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use an AWS CloudHSM cluster, and have the application call the HSMs through the CloudHSM PKCS #11 library.",
      "Use AWS KMS keys with imported key material, and have the application call AWS KMS through a PKCS #11 library.",
      "Use AWS KMS keys in a CloudHSM key store, and have the application call AWS KMS through a PKCS #11 library.",
      "Use AWS KMS keys in an external key store, and have the application call AWS KMS through a PKCS #11 library."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudHSM gives the company single-tenant HSMs whose users and keys it controls, and its PKCS #11 library lets the application call the HSMs directly.",
      "Imported key material lives in AWS KMS HSMs, which are multi-tenant and managed by AWS, and AWS KMS has no PKCS #11 interface.",
      "A CloudHSM key store is used through the AWS KMS API, not through PKCS #11.",
      "An external key store keeps keys outside AWS, and the application would still call the AWS KMS API, not PKCS #11."] },

{ id: "d5-15", d: 5,
  q: "A company must encrypt Amazon S3 and Amazon EBS data with keys whose key material is generated in, and never leaves, single-tenant HSMs that the company controls in AWS. The company wants to keep using the native AWS KMS integration of Amazon S3 and Amazon EBS and does not want to change any application.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an AWS CloudHSM key store in AWS KMS backed by a CloudHSM cluster, and create KMS keys in it.",
      "Generate the key material in a CloudHSM cluster, and import it into KMS keys that have the EXTERNAL origin.",
      "Create an external key store in AWS KMS that connects through a proxy to the company's on-premises HSMs.",
      "Use client-side encryption with keys from a CloudHSM cluster, and store the encrypted data in S3 and EBS."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["KMS keys in a CloudHSM key store have their key material generated and used in the company's CloudHSM cluster, and they work with KMS-integrated services such as S3 and EBS.",
      "Importing the material copies it out of the CloudHSM cluster into AWS KMS.",
      "An external key store uses HSMs outside AWS, not HSMs that the company controls in AWS.",
      "Client-side encryption gives up the native KMS integration and requires application changes."] },

{ id: "d5-16", d: 5,
  q: "An application stores customer records in Amazon S3. A new rule requires that records are encrypted inside the application before they leave it, so the plaintext is never sent to AWS, even over TLS. The keys must be protected by AWS KMS, and each record must be encrypted under its own data key.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use the AWS Encryption SDK with an AWS KMS keyring to encrypt each record in the application before upload.",
      "Upload each record with SSE-KMS by using a customer managed key and the x-amz-server-side-encryption header.",
      "Upload each record with SSE-C, and send a data key from AWS KMS in the request headers of each upload.",
      "Call the AWS KMS Encrypt API on each record, and upload the ciphertext with the KMS key ID in the metadata."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The AWS Encryption SDK performs client-side envelope encryption. Each record gets its own data key, which is protected by the KMS key, and only ciphertext leaves the application.",
      "With SSE-KMS, S3 receives the plaintext and encrypts it on the server side.",
      "With SSE-C, S3 receives the plaintext and the key, and encrypts the data on the server side.",
      "KMS Encrypt handles at most 4 KB and uses no data keys, so it is not envelope encryption for each record."] },

{ id: "d5-17", d: 5,
  q: "An Amazon S3 bucket uses default encryption with SSE-KMS and a customer managed key. The bucket receives about 5 million uploads each day, the AWS KMS request costs are high, and some uploads are throttled by the KMS request quota. Objects must stay encrypted with the same customer managed key.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Bucket Keys for the bucket's default SSE-KMS encryption.",
      "Change the bucket's default encryption to SSE-S3 to remove the KMS requests.",
      "Change the bucket's default encryption to DSSE-KMS with the same customer key.",
      "Request a higher KMS request quota, and keep the current encryption settings."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 Bucket Keys make S3 use a bucket-level key from KMS to create data keys, which cuts the requests from S3 to KMS and their cost while keeping the same customer managed key.",
      "SSE-S3 stops using the customer managed key, which the requirement does not allow.",
      "Dual-layer encryption makes more KMS requests and does not support S3 Bucket Keys.",
      "A higher quota reduces throttling but not the request costs."] },

{ id: "d5-18", d: 5,
  q: "A company must ensure that every new Amazon EBS volume in its account in us-east-1 is encrypted with a specific customer managed KMS key. This includes volumes that teams create from unencrypted snapshots and volumes that teams forget to mark as encrypted. Teams create volumes through the console, the AWS CLI, and AWS CloudFormation.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on EBS encryption by default in us-east-1, and set the customer managed key as the default EBS encryption key.",
      "Turn on EBS encryption by default in us-east-1, and keep the AWS managed key aws/ebs as the default encryption key.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule for unencrypted EBS volumes, with an automatic remediation that deletes the volumes.",
      "Add an IAM policy for each team that requires the ec2:Encrypted condition key on every ec2:CreateVolume call."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Encryption by default encrypts every new volume in the Region, including volumes created from unencrypted snapshots, with the default key that the account sets.",
      "The volumes would be encrypted with aws/ebs, not with the required customer managed key.",
      "Deleting volumes after creation loses data and does not encrypt anything.",
      "The condition requires encryption but not the specific key, and administrators can change their own policies."] },

{ id: "d5-19", d: 5,
  q: "An existing Amazon EC2 instance has an unencrypted 500 GB Amazon EBS data volume. A security policy now requires the volume to be encrypted with a customer managed AWS KMS key. The volume holds a reporting database whose data must be kept, and a short maintenance window is available for the instance.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Snapshot the volume, create an encrypted volume from the snapshot with the KMS key, and swap the volumes in the window.",
      "Modify the existing volume during the window to turn on encryption with the KMS key, and keep it attached to the instance.",
      "Turn on EBS encryption by default with the KMS key, and reboot the instance in the window to encrypt the attached volume.",
      "Snapshot the volume, change the snapshot's encryption setting to the KMS key in place, and restore the volume from it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An unencrypted volume cannot be encrypted in place. A new volume created from its snapshot with encryption turned on uses the KMS key, and swapping the volumes in the window completes the change.",
      "EBS does not support turning on encryption for an existing volume.",
      "Encryption by default affects only new volumes. A reboot does not encrypt an existing one.",
      "A snapshot's encryption cannot be changed in place. It must be copied or used to create a new encrypted volume."] },

{ id: "d5-20", d: 5,
  q: "A financial company must keep trade records in Amazon S3 for 7 years in a write once, read many (WORM) model for a securities regulation. No user, including the root user, may delete or overwrite a record during the retention period, and new records must be protected automatically when they are written.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Object Lock for the bucket, and set a default retention period of 7 years in compliance mode.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock for the bucket, and set a default retention period of 7 years in governance mode.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock for the bucket, and place a legal hold on each record after the record is written.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning and MFA delete for the bucket, and deny s3:DeleteObject in the bucket policy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Default retention protects each new object as it is written. In compliance mode, no user, including the root user, can delete or overwrite a protected version or shorten the retention.",
      "Users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission can delete objects in governance mode.",
      "Legal holds are placed by hand, have no end date, and can be removed by users with permission.",
      "The root user can change the bucket policy and delete versions with the MFA device, so this is not WORM."] },

{ id: "d5-21", d: 5,
  q: "A company archives records in an Amazon S3 Glacier vault. A regulation requires a vault policy that denies deletion of any archive younger than 10 years, and the policy must be impossible to change once it is in force. The company wants a chance to test the policy before it becomes permanent.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Initiate an S3 Glacier Vault Lock with the deny policy, test it during the 24-hour in-progress state, and then complete the lock.",
      "Attach a vault access policy with the deny statement, and remove every IAM permission to change vault access policies.",
      "Initiate an S3 Glacier Vault Lock with the deny policy, and leave the lock in the in-progress state to keep testing it.",
      "Encrypt the archives with a customer managed KMS key, and deny kms:ScheduleKeyDeletion in the key policy for 10 years."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Initiating a Vault Lock puts the policy in an in-progress state for 24 hours for testing. Completing the lock makes the policy immutable.",
      "A vault access policy can be changed again by anyone who regains the permission, so it is not immutable.",
      "An in-progress lock expires after 24 hours and is never made permanent.",
      "Protecting the key does not stop anyone from deleting archives."] },

{ id: "d5-22", d: 5,
  q: "A company deploys about 40 AWS Lambda functions from a CI/CD pipeline. The company must ensure that only code that its build system has signed can be deployed to its AWS Lambda functions. Deployments of unsigned code packages, or of packages that changed after signing, must fail rather than only raise a warning.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an AWS Signer signing profile for Lambda, and sign each code package in the build system with it.",
      "Attach a code signing configuration that allows the profile, with the untrusted artifact policy set to Enforce.",
      "Attach a code signing configuration that allows the profile, with the untrusted artifact policy set to Warn.",
      "Allow only the build system's role to upload packages to the S3 bucket that stores the Lambda code.",
      "Turn on Amazon Inspector Lambda code scanning, and roll back the functions that have critical findings."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Signer signing profiles sign Lambda code packages, so the build system can sign each package it produces.",
      "A code signing configuration checks that each deployment is signed by an allowed profile and unchanged. With Enforce, deployments that fail the check are rejected.",
      "With Warn, Lambda logs a warning but still deploys unsigned or changed code.",
      "Limiting uploads to the bucket does not stop deployments of code from other places, and Lambda does not check signatures.",
      "Scanning finds vulnerabilities after deployment. It does not check signatures or block deployments."] },

{ id: "d5-23", d: 5,
  q: "A company uploads large data files to Amazon S3 from its data center. A compliance rule requires proof that each stored object is exactly what the client sent, by using a SHA-256 checksum that S3 validates during the upload and keeps with the object for later checks.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Upload with the SHA-256 checksum algorithm and the client's checksum, which S3 checks and stores with the object.",
      "Upload with the Content-MD5 header, which S3 validates during the upload and keeps as the object's SHA-256 checksum.",
      "Upload normally, and compare the object's ETag with a SHA-256 hash that the client calculates before the upload.",
      "Upload with SSE-KMS encryption, which detects changes to the data and records a SHA-256 checksum for each object."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 supports additional checksum algorithms, including SHA-256. S3 validates the checksum that the client sends, rejects a mismatch, and stores the checksum with the object.",
      "Content-MD5 is an MD5 check, not SHA-256, and S3 does not store it as a SHA-256 checksum.",
      "An ETag is not a SHA-256 hash, and for multipart uploads it is not a hash of the whole object.",
      "Encryption protects confidentiality. It does not validate or record a SHA-256 checksum."] },

{ id: "d5-24", d: 5,
  q: "An Amazon S3 bucket with S3 Versioning receives about 50 GB of application logs every day from 200 servers. Logs must stay in S3 Standard for 90 days and then move to S3 Glacier Deep Archive. Every version of every log must be deleted 7 years after creation, without manual work.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a lifecycle rule that transitions current versions to Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days, expires them after 7 years, and deletes noncurrent versions.",
      "Add a lifecycle rule that transitions current versions to Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days and expires the current versions after 7 years only.",
      "Turn on S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Deep Archive Access tier, and delete the logs by hand when they become 7 years old.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock with a 7-year retention period in governance mode, which deletes the logs when the retention ends."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Lifecycle rules transition and expire objects on a schedule. In a versioned bucket, expiring the current version only adds a delete marker, so the rule must also delete noncurrent versions.",
      "Without a noncurrent version action, the old versions stay forever.",
      "Intelligent-Tiering moves objects by access pattern, not after a fixed 90 days, and manual deletion is not allowed.",
      "Object Lock prevents deletion during retention. It does not delete anything when the retention ends."] },

{ id: "d5-25", d: 5,
  q: "A company has a 4 TB Amazon EFS file system that was created without encryption at rest and is mounted by 20 instances. A security policy now requires that the data is stored on an EFS file system that is encrypted at rest with a customer managed KMS key, with as little manual copying work as possible.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a new EFS file system with encryption at rest turned on and the customer managed KMS key.",
      "Use an AWS DataSync task to copy the data from the old file system to the new one, then switch the mounts.",
      "Turn on encryption at rest in the existing file system's settings, and select the customer managed key.",
      "Back up the file system with AWS Backup, and restore the backup into the same file system with encryption.",
      "Mount the existing file system with the tls option, which encrypts the stored data on the next write."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Encryption at rest for EFS is chosen when the file system is created and cannot be turned on later, so a new encrypted file system is needed.",
      "DataSync copies the data between the file systems as a managed task, which avoids manual copying.",
      "Encryption at rest cannot be turned on for an existing file system.",
      "Restoring into the same file system keeps it unencrypted, because encryption is fixed at creation.",
      "The tls option encrypts data in transit, not data at rest."] },

{ id: "d5-26", d: 5,
  q: "A research team uses an Amazon FSx for Lustre scratch file system for 40 TB of results that take weeks to compute. The team now needs automatic daily backups of the file system that are kept for 30 days, and the data must survive the failure of a file server.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Move the data to a persistent FSx for Lustre file system, and turn on automatic daily backups with 30-day retention.",
      "Turn on automatic daily backups for the existing scratch file system, and set the backup retention period to 30 days.",
      "Keep the scratch file system, and add a data repository association that exports changes to Amazon S3 every day.",
      "Move the data to a persistent FSx for Lustre file system, and create one user-initiated backup at the end of each month."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Scratch file systems do not support backups and do not replicate data, so a file server failure can lose data. Persistent file systems replicate data and support automatic daily backups with a retention period.",
      "Backups are not supported on scratch file systems.",
      "An export is a copy of changed files, not a backup with retention, and the scratch file system still loses data on a server failure.",
      "A monthly user-initiated backup is not a daily backup."] },

{ id: "d5-27", d: 5,
  q: "A company must take daily snapshots of all Amazon EBS volumes that have the tag backup=daily in one account, keep 14 days of snapshots, and copy each snapshot to a second Region for disaster recovery. The copies must be encrypted with a KMS key in that Region, and the solution must not require custom code.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policy that targets the tag, runs daily, keeps 14 snapshots, and copies them to the second Region with a KMS key there.",
      "Create an Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policy that targets the tag, runs daily, and keeps 14 snapshots. Turn on EBS encryption by default in the second Region.",
      "Create an AWS Backup plan with a daily backup rule for the tagged volumes and a 14-day retention period, and keep its copy settings at their default values.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge Scheduler schedule that calls the CreateSnapshot and CopySnapshot APIs every day for each volume that has the tag."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Data Lifecycle Manager policy snapshots volumes with the target tag on a schedule, keeps a set number of snapshots, and can copy each one to another Region with a KMS key there, without code.",
      "Encryption by default does not copy any snapshots to the second Region.",
      "Without a copy rule, the backup plan keeps all recovery points in one Region.",
      "Scheduled API calls do not delete old snapshots after 14 days, and they must list each volume."] },

{ id: "d5-28", d: 5,
  q: "A company uses AWS Backup for its production account. After a ransomware incident at another company, the security team wants recovery points that no one can delete before their retention ends, including an attacker who takes over the production account. The recovery points must stay available if the production account is compromised.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode to the backup vault in the production account.",
      "Copy the recovery points to a locked vault in a separate backup account in the organization.",
      "Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in governance mode to the backup vault in the production account.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning and MFA delete on the backup vault in the production account.",
      "Encrypt the recovery points with a customer managed KMS key in the production account."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["In compliance mode, after the grace time, the lock cannot be removed and recovery points cannot be deleted before their retention ends, even by the root user.",
      "Copies in a separate account stay available and protected when the production account is compromised.",
      "In governance mode, users with the right IAM permissions can remove the lock and delete recovery points.",
      "Backup vaults are not S3 buckets, so versioning and MFA delete do not apply.",
      "A key in the compromised account does not stop an attacker from deleting the recovery points."] },

{ id: "d5-29", d: 5,
  q: "A company must move 50 TB of files from an on-premises NFS server to Amazon S3 over its AWS Direct Connect connection. The data must be encrypted in transit and must not traverse the public internet. The company must be able to show that every file arrived without corruption.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use AWS DataSync with an on-premises agent, a DataSync interface VPC endpoint reached over Direct Connect, and data integrity verification.",
      "Use AWS DataSync with an on-premises agent over the public internet, and turn off data integrity verification to finish the transfer faster.",
      "Use an AWS Snowball Edge device to ship the files to AWS, and run a checksum script on the S3 objects after the import is complete.",
      "Copy the files with a custom FTP script over the Direct Connect connection, and compare the file counts on both sides after the copy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["DataSync encrypts traffic with TLS, can reach AWS through an interface VPC endpoint over Direct Connect, and verifies data integrity during and after the transfer.",
      "This sends the traffic over the internet and removes the integrity proof.",
      "A Snowball device does not move the data over Direct Connect, and a later checksum script does not compare against the source.",
      "FTP is not encrypted, and file counts do not show that the contents are intact."] },

{ id: "d5-30", d: 5,
  q: "A company replicates an Amazon S3 bucket in us-east-1 to a bucket in us-west-2 for disaster recovery. Objects encrypted with SSE-S3 replicate, but objects encrypted with SSE-KMS by using a customer managed key in us-east-1 do not. The replicas must be encrypted with a customer managed key in us-west-2.\n\nWhich combination of steps will resolve this issue? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Turn on replication of objects encrypted with AWS KMS in the rule, and set the us-west-2 key as the replica key.",
      "Allow the replication role to use kms:Decrypt on the source key and kms:Encrypt on the destination key.",
      "Allow the replication role to use kms:Decrypt on the source key only, because replicas use SSE-S3.",
      "Turn on S3 Bucket Keys on the destination bucket, which lets S3 replicate SSE-KMS objects without keys.",
      "Limit the replication rule to objects with a tag, and add that tag to every SSE-KMS encrypted object."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["S3 does not replicate SSE-KMS objects unless the rule turns this on, and the rule names the key that encrypts the replicas in the destination Region.",
      "The replication role decrypts with the source key and encrypts with the destination key, so it needs both permissions.",
      "Replicas must use the us-west-2 customer managed key, so the role also needs to encrypt with it.",
      "Bucket Keys reduce KMS requests. They do not remove the need for replication settings or key permissions.",
      "Tags do not change how S3 replication handles SSE-KMS objects."] },

{ id: "d5-31", d: 5,
  q: "A company stores regulated documents in an Amazon S3 bucket that several application teams write to. Every upload must request SSE-KMS with one specific customer managed key. Uploads that request a different KMS key, request SSE-S3, or send no encryption headers must be rejected, including uploads from principals whose IAM policies allow s3:PutObject.\n\nWhich bucket policy statement will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Deny s3:PutObject when s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption-aws-kms-key-id does not equal the key's ARN, with StringNotEquals.",
      "Deny s3:PutObject when s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption-aws-kms-key-id does not equal the key's ARN, with StringNotEquals and a Null condition set to false.",
      "Deny s3:PutObject when s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption does not equal aws:kms, with StringNotEquals.",
      "Allow s3:PutObject only when s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption-aws-kms-key-id equals the key's ARN, with StringEquals."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A negated operator such as StringNotEquals is true when the key ID header is missing, so the deny rejects other keys, SSE-S3 uploads, and uploads with no encryption headers, for every principal.",
      "A Null condition set to false limits the deny to requests that include a key ID header, so SSE-S3 uploads and uploads with no headers are not denied.",
      "This rejects SSE-S3 and unencrypted requests but still allows any other KMS key.",
      "An allow statement does not stop principals whose IAM policies already allow s3:PutObject."] },

{ id: "d5-32", d: 5,
  q: "A company stores critical files in a versioned Amazon S3 bucket. The security team wants an extra control that requires an MFA code from the root user's device to permanently delete an object version or to suspend versioning, in addition to the IAM permissions for those actions. Five administrators currently have those permissions.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Turn on MFA delete for the bucket with the root user's credentials and MFA device, by using the AWS CLI or the API.",
      "Turn on MFA delete for the bucket in the S3 console, by using an IAM user that has administrator access and its own MFA device.",
      "Add a bucket policy that denies s3:DeleteObjectVersion and s3:PutBucketVersioning when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is false.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock in governance mode for the bucket, and give the root user's MFA device to the security team to hold."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["MFA delete requires the bucket owner's MFA code to permanently delete a version or change the versioning state. It can be turned on only by the root user, through the AWS CLI or the API.",
      "Only the root user can turn on MFA delete, and the S3 console does not support turning it on.",
      "This requires any MFA session, not a code from the root user's device.",
      "Governance mode is bypassed with an IAM permission and is not tied to the root user's device."] },

{ id: "d5-33", d: 5,
  q: "An application calls a third-party payment API with an API key that is stored in AWS Secrets Manager. The provider lets customers create and revoke keys through its own REST API, and a key stays valid until it is revoked. The company must rotate the key every 30 days automatically, with no period in which the application has no valid key.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on rotation with a custom Lambda rotation function that creates a new provider key, tests it, makes it AWSCURRENT, and then revokes the old key.",
      "Turn on managed rotation for the secret, and let Secrets Manager call the provider's REST API to create the new key and revoke the old key.",
      "Turn on rotation with a custom Lambda rotation function that revokes the old provider key first, and then creates and stores the new key.",
      "Create an EventBridge schedule every 30 days that runs a Lambda function to store a new provider key in the secret without revoking old keys."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A rotation function runs the create, set, test, and finish steps. Creating and testing the new key before it becomes current, and revoking the old key last, keeps a valid key available the whole time.",
      "Managed rotation works only for secrets that other AWS services manage, not for third-party API keys.",
      "Revoking the old key before the new key is stored leaves the application without a valid key.",
      "Old keys stay valid forever, so this is not rotation of the credential."] },

{ id: "d5-34", d: 5,
  q: "Secrets Manager rotation fails for a database secret. The rotation Lambda function runs in private subnets of the database's VPC, which have no NAT gateway, and the function can reach the database. Its logs show timeouts when it calls the Secrets Manager API, and the VPC has DNS hostnames and DNS resolution turned on.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Create a Secrets Manager interface VPC endpoint with private DNS in the function's subnets, and allow HTTPS from the function's security group.",
      "Create a Secrets Manager gateway VPC endpoint, and add the endpoint to the route tables of the subnets that the rotation function uses.",
      "Move the rotation function out of the VPC, which gives it a path to the Secrets Manager API and to the database in the private subnets.",
      "Increase the rotation function's timeout to 15 minutes, and start the rotation of the secret again from the Secrets Manager console."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The function needs a private path to the Secrets Manager API. An interface endpoint with private DNS gives it one under the normal endpoint name, and its security group must allow HTTPS from the function.",
      "Secrets Manager has only interface endpoints, not gateway endpoints.",
      "Outside the VPC, the function can no longer reach the database in the private subnets.",
      "A longer timeout does not create a network path, so the calls still time out."] },

{ id: "d5-35", d: 5,
  q: "An AWS Lambda function reads a database password from AWS Secrets Manager on every invocation, about 2,000 times each second at peak. Costs and latency are high, and some GetSecretValue calls are throttled. The function must pick up a rotated password within 5 minutes after rotation without a redeployment.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add the AWS Parameters and Secrets Lambda Extension, read the secret through its local cache, and set the cache TTL to 300 seconds.",
      "Add the AWS Parameters and Secrets Lambda Extension, read the secret through its local cache, and set the cache TTL to 86,400 seconds.",
      "Read the secret once during each deployment, and store the password in an encrypted environment variable of the function.",
      "Keep calling GetSecretValue on every invocation, and request a higher Secrets Manager API request quota for the account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The extension caches the secret inside the execution environment, which removes most API calls and their cost and latency. A 300-second TTL picks up a rotated password within 5 minutes.",
      "A 24-hour TTL can keep the old password for a day after rotation.",
      "An environment variable changes only on redeployment, which the requirement rules out.",
      "A higher quota reduces throttling but not the cost or latency of every call."] },

{ id: "d5-36", d: 5,
  q: "A company runs an application in us-east-1 with a disaster recovery copy in us-west-2. The application reads 40 credentials from AWS Secrets Manager, and most of them rotate every 30 days. During a Regional outage, the recovery copy must read the same secrets locally in us-west-2, and rotated values must reach us-west-2 without extra work.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Replicate the secrets to us-west-2 with Secrets Manager multi-Region secrets, and have the recovery copy read the replicas.",
      "Create matching secrets in us-west-2, and turn on separate rotation for them on the same schedule as us-east-1.",
      "Have the recovery copy read the secrets in us-east-1 through an interface VPC endpoint during the outage.",
      "Export the secrets to an encrypted S3 bucket in us-west-2 each night, and have the recovery copy read them there."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Replica secrets are kept in sync with the primary, including rotated values, so the recovery copy reads current secrets locally in us-west-2.",
      "Separate rotation creates different values in each Region, so the two sides no longer match.",
      "The recovery copy would depend on us-east-1, which may be the Region that is down.",
      "Nightly exports are out of date after a rotation and create another copy of the secrets to protect."] },

{ id: "d5-37", d: 5,
  q: "A regulation requires a company to generate its AWS KMS key material in its own on-premises HSM and to use that material for AWS KMS encryption of resources in AWS. The key material must be protected while it is transferred to AWS. A security engineer will do the setup from a workstation that can reach both the HSM and the AWS KMS API.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select THREE.)",
  o: ["Create a KMS key with no key material by choosing the External (Import key material) origin.",
      "Download the wrapping public key and the import token for the KMS key from AWS KMS.",
      "Encrypt the key material with the wrapping public key, and import it with the import token.",
      "Create a KMS key with the AWS KMS origin, and replace its key material with the on-premises material.",
      "Upload the plaintext key material to AWS Secrets Manager, and point a new KMS key at the secret."],
  a: [0, 1, 2],
  r: ["A KMS key with the EXTERNAL origin is created without key material, ready for the company's own material.",
      "AWS KMS provides a wrapping public key and an import token for that key.",
      "Encrypting the material with the wrapping key protects it in transit, and the import token lets AWS KMS accept it.",
      "The key material of a key with the AWS_KMS origin cannot be replaced.",
      "KMS keys cannot use material from a secret, and the material would be stored in plaintext form."] },

{ id: "d5-38", d: 5,
  q: "A company wants to be able to make a KMS key unusable immediately during a security incident, and later restore the same key, after which data encrypted under it can be decrypted again. Scheduling key deletion is not acceptable because it is permanent, and disabling the key is not enough because any administrator with kms:EnableKey could enable it again.\n\nWhich approach will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use a KMS key with imported key material, delete the key material when needed, and import the same material again later.",
      "Use a KMS key with AWS generated key material, disable it during an incident, and enable it again afterward.",
      "Use a KMS key with AWS generated key material, and turn on automatic rotation during an incident to replace its material.",
      "Use a KMS key in the default key store, and export its key material to the company's HSM before an incident."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Deleting imported key material makes the key unusable at once, and no one can restore it without the company's copy of the material. Importing the same material later makes the old data decryptable again.",
      "Any administrator with kms:EnableKey can enable the key again, which the company does not accept.",
      "Rotation keeps the older material for decryption, so the key stays usable.",
      "Key material generated by AWS KMS cannot be exported."] },

{ id: "d5-39", d: 5,
  q: "A company's regulator requires that encryption keys for certain workloads are stored and used only in the company's own key manager outside AWS. The company must be able to stop all use of the keys at any time by disconnecting its key manager, and still wants AWS KMS integration with services such as Amazon S3.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an external key store in AWS KMS that connects through an external key store proxy to the company's key manager.",
      "Create KMS keys with imported key material from the company's key manager, and delete the local copies after the import.",
      "Create an AWS CloudHSM key store in AWS KMS, and keep the company's key manager as a backup of the key material.",
      "Use client-side encryption with keys from the company's key manager, and upload the encrypted data to Amazon S3."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["KMS keys in an external key store keep their key material in the company's key manager, and every cryptographic operation goes to it through the proxy. Disconnecting the key manager stops all use of the keys.",
      "Imported key material is stored and used inside AWS KMS.",
      "A CloudHSM key store keeps the keys in AWS.",
      "Client-side encryption gives up the AWS KMS integration of services such as Amazon S3."] },

{ id: "d5-40", d: 5,
  q: "A company's policy requires that the key material of its 60 customer managed AWS KMS keys is rotated every 180 days, without changing key IDs or ARNs. The keys protect data in Amazon S3, Amazon EBS, and Amazon RDS, and applications refer to them by key ARN. Data encrypted under older key material must remain decryptable without re-encryption.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on automatic key rotation for each key with a rotation period of 180 days.",
      "Turn on automatic key rotation for each key, which rotates key material every 365 days.",
      "Create a new KMS key every 180 days, and point each key's alias to the new key.",
      "Schedule each key for deletion every 180 days, and create a new key with the same alias."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automatic rotation accepts a rotation period from 90 to 2,560 days and keeps the same key ID and ARN. AWS KMS keeps older key material, so old data stays decryptable.",
      "The rotation period can be set, so 365 days is not required, and it misses the 180-day policy.",
      "New keys have new key IDs and ARNs, which the policy does not allow.",
      "Deleting keys makes the data encrypted under them unrecoverable."] },

{ id: "d5-41", d: 5,
  q: "An application encrypts data with AWS KMS in us-east-1 and stores it in an Amazon DynamoDB global table that replicates to eu-west-1. The application encrypts each record on the client side before it writes the record to the table. During a Regional failover, the application in eu-west-1 must decrypt the replicated data without any call to AWS KMS in us-east-1.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Encrypt with a multi-Region primary key in us-east-1, and create a replica key in eu-west-1 for the application.",
      "Encrypt with a single-Region key in us-east-1, and create a key with the same alias in eu-west-1.",
      "Encrypt with a single-Region key in us-east-1, and grant the eu-west-1 application kms:Decrypt on it.",
      "Encrypt with a multi-Region primary key in us-east-1, and have eu-west-1 keep calling the primary key."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Multi-Region keys share key material and key ID across Regions. The replica in eu-west-1 decrypts data that the primary key encrypted, with no call to us-east-1.",
      "A key with the same alias has different key material, so it cannot decrypt the data.",
      "Decrypting with a us-east-1 key always calls AWS KMS in us-east-1.",
      "Calling the primary key depends on us-east-1, which the failover must avoid."] },

{ id: "d5-42", d: 5,
  q: "A customer managed KMS key is used for SSE-KMS on an Amazon S3 bucket in us-east-1 that holds financial reports. The analysts work from IAM roles in the same account. They must be able to read the objects through Amazon S3, but must not be able to call AWS KMS directly to decrypt data with the key.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["In the key policy statement that lets the analysts use the key, add kms:ViaService with the value s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com.",
      "In the key policy statement that lets the analysts use the key, add kms:CallerAccount with the bucket owner's 12-digit account ID.",
      "Remove kms:Decrypt from the analysts' permissions, and allow them only s3:GetObject on the objects in the bucket.",
      "Turn on S3 Bucket Keys for the bucket, which blocks direct calls to AWS KMS with this key from every principal."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["kms:ViaService limits use of the key to requests that come through the named service in the Region. S3 reads work, and direct calls to AWS KMS from the analysts do not match.",
      "kms:CallerAccount checks the caller's account, which is the same for direct calls and calls through S3.",
      "S3 decrypts SSE-KMS objects with the caller's permissions, so without kms:Decrypt the reads fail.",
      "Bucket Keys reduce KMS requests. They do not block direct calls."] },

{ id: "d5-43", d: 5,
  q: "A multi-tenant application encrypts every tenant's data with one AWS KMS key, and each tenant's workers use a separate IAM role. Each role must be able to decrypt only data that was encrypted for its own tenant, and the tenant for each decryption must appear in AWS CloudTrail.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Encrypt with the tenant ID in the encryption context, and allow each role kms:Decrypt only when kms:EncryptionContext:tenant matches its tenant.",
      "Encrypt with the tenant ID in the encryption context, and allow each role kms:Decrypt with no condition on the encryption context.",
      "Encrypt with a separate grant token for each tenant, and allow each role kms:Decrypt on the key with no other conditions.",
      "Store the tenant ID in each object's S3 metadata, and allow each role kms:Decrypt when that metadata matches its tenant."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Encryption context is bound to the ciphertext and recorded in CloudTrail. A kms:EncryptionContext condition for the tenant lets each role decrypt only its own tenant's data.",
      "Without a condition, every role can decrypt every tenant's data.",
      "Grant tokens let a new grant work right away. They do not tie ciphertext to a tenant.",
      "AWS KMS does not evaluate S3 object metadata in its conditions."] },

{ id: "d5-44", d: 5,
  q: "Application logs in 30 Amazon CloudWatch Logs log groups sometimes contain customers' email addresses and credit card numbers. Development teams create new log groups every week as they release new microservices. The security team must mask this data automatically in all current and future log groups in the account, without changing the applications.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an account-level CloudWatch Logs data protection policy with managed data identifiers for email addresses and credit card numbers.",
      "Create a CloudWatch Logs data protection policy on each of the 30 log groups that uses the managed data identifiers for these data types.",
      "Create a metric filter in every log group for each sensitive data type, and alarm whenever the applications write sensitive data to the logs.",
      "Encrypt all of the log groups with an AWS KMS key, and allow only the security team to use the key for decrypting the log events."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An account-level data protection policy applies to all log groups in the account, including new ones, and masks the matching data without application changes.",
      "Policies on the 30 log groups miss log groups that are created later.",
      "Metric filters count matches. They do not mask the data.",
      "Encryption at rest does not mask anything for readers who can read the logs."] },

{ id: "d5-45", d: 5,
  q: "An application publishes order messages to an Amazon SNS topic that has five subscribers: an analytics service, a shipping partner's HTTPS endpoint, and three internal Amazon SQS queues. Some subscribers must never receive credit card numbers, but the publisher sometimes puts them in message bodies. The publisher cannot be changed, and every order must still be delivered.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Attach an SNS message data protection policy to the topic that de-identifies credit card numbers in outbound messages.",
      "Attach an SNS message data protection policy to the topic that denies inbound messages that contain credit card numbers.",
      "Add a subscription filter policy for each subscriber that drops messages whose body contains credit card numbers.",
      "Encrypt the topic with a customer managed KMS key, and deny kms:Decrypt to the subscribers that must not see card numbers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Message data protection policies find sensitive data such as credit card numbers and can mask or redact it on delivery, so every order still arrives without the card numbers.",
      "Denying inbound messages blocks those orders, which must still be delivered.",
      "Dropping messages loses orders, and filter policies do not detect card numbers.",
      "Subscribers that cannot decrypt would receive nothing, so orders would not be delivered to them."] },

{ id: "d5-46", d: 5,
  q: "A log group for a payment application has a CloudWatch Logs data protection policy that masks personal data such as names and card numbers. During an investigation, two authorized analysts must see the unmasked log events in CloudWatch Logs Insights. Every other user, including other administrators of the log group, must keep seeing masked data.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Grant the two analysts logs:Unmask on the log group, and have them use the unmask option when they query.",
      "Remove the data protection policy from the log group during the investigation, and add it back after the investigation.",
      "Grant the two analysts kms:Decrypt on the log group's KMS key, which shows the unmasked data in their queries.",
      "Grant the two analysts logs:GetLogEvents and logs:FilterLogEvents, which return the original data for any query."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Only principals with logs:Unmask can see masked data in its original form. Granting it to the two analysts keeps the data masked for everyone else.",
      "Removing the policy unmasks the data for every user, including the other administrators.",
      "The log group's KMS key protects data at rest. It does not control masking.",
      "These read permissions return masked data unless the caller also has logs:Unmask."] },

{ id: "d5-47", d: 5,
  q: "A company has a subordinate CA in AWS Private Certificate Authority in a shared security account. Application teams in 60 accounts of the organization must request private certificates from this CA through ACM in their own accounts, with automatic renewal, and without any access to the security account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Share the CA with the organization by using AWS RAM, and have the teams request private certificates from the shared CA through ACM.",
      "Create a cross-account IAM role in the security account that the teams assume to issue certificates from the CA.",
      "Create a separate subordinate CA in each of the 60 accounts, signed by the shared CA, for each team to use.",
      "Export the CA certificate and its private key, and import them into ACM in each account to issue certificates."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS RAM can share a private CA with the organization. Teams then request certificates from it through ACM in their own accounts, and ACM renews them.",
      "Assuming a role in the security account gives the teams access to that account.",
      "Sixty more CAs work, but they add cost and many CAs to run.",
      "A CA's private key cannot be exported from AWS Private CA."] },

{ id: "d5-48", d: 5,
  q: "A security engineer requests a public certificate in AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) in eu-west-1 for www.example.com, which is served through Amazon CloudFront. The certificate is issued, but it does not appear in the list of certificates when the engineer edits the distribution's settings. All of the company's other resources run in eu-west-1.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Request or import the certificate in ACM in US East (N. Virginia), and select it for the distribution.",
      "Turn on CloudFront Origin Shield in eu-west-1, which lets the distribution use certificates from that Region.",
      "Wait 24 hours, because new ACM certificates appear in CloudFront only after the next certificate sync.",
      "Request the certificate again in eu-west-1 with email validation instead of DNS validation, and select it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront uses ACM certificates only from the US East (N. Virginia) Region, so the certificate must be requested or imported there.",
      "Origin Shield is a caching layer. It does not change where CloudFront looks for certificates.",
      "No delay will make a eu-west-1 certificate appear for CloudFront.",
      "The validation method does not matter. The Region does."] },

{ id: "d5-49", d: 5,
  q: "A company plans to delete a customer managed KMS key that it believes none of its 30 applications use anymore. The company must keep the chance to stop the deletion if anything still uses the key, and must be told right away if anything tries to use the key before the deletion completes.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Schedule the key deletion with the maximum waiting period of 30 days.",
      "Create a CloudWatch alarm from CloudTrail events for attempts to use the key pending deletion.",
      "Schedule the key deletion with the minimum waiting period of 7 days to finish sooner.",
      "Disable the key, and delete it right away if no application fails within one day.",
      "Turn on automatic key rotation, and delete the key after the next rotation finishes."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Deletion always has a waiting period of 7 to 30 days in which it can be canceled. The longest period gives the most time to find a user of the key.",
      "Use of a key pending deletion fails and is recorded in CloudTrail. An alarm on those events, as the AWS KMS documentation describes, warns the company in time to cancel.",
      "A shorter waiting period leaves less time to find users, and nothing notifies the company.",
      "AWS KMS keys cannot be deleted right away. Deletion always has a waiting period.",
      "Rotation does not delete keys or show whether a key is still in use."] },

{ id: "d5-50", d: 5,
  q: "A company's key policy for a customer managed KMS key gives one IAM role full access to the key. The security team wants separation of duties: the security administrators who manage the key must not be able to use it to decrypt data, and the application role that uses the key must not be able to change its policy or schedule its deletion.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Split the key policy into a key administrators statement with management actions for the admins and a key users statement with cryptographic actions for the application.",
      "Keep one key policy statement that gives kms:* to the admins and the application role, and turn on AWS CloudTrail to review every use of the key afterward.",
      "Give the admins kms:* in the key policy, and give the application role kms:Encrypt and kms:Decrypt in the key policy, with no other changes to the policy.",
      "Give the admins the key management actions in the key policy, and give the application role kms:* on the key in its own IAM identity policy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The default key policy pattern separates key administrators, who manage the key but cannot use it for cryptography, from key users, who use the key but cannot manage it.",
      "Logging records actions but does not separate the duties.",
      "kms:* lets the admins decrypt data with the key.",
      "kms:* in the application role's identity policy lets it change the key policy and schedule deletion when the key policy allows IAM policies."] },
]);
